# One line ending everywhere. The corpus is compared byte for byte against what the example
# that writes it produces, and a Windows checkout that turned its line feeds into CRLF
# would make that comparison fail on that platform alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.xlsx binary
*.ods binary

# What a `git archive` of this repository leaves out. Packagist serves the Composer package
# as GitHub's archive of the tag, so without this every `composer require` would download
# every binding, the Rust core and the corpus to use one of them. Composer needs
# composer.json, php/src (the sources and php/src/native), LICENSE and the README. A
# deny-list, as in HyperCast: a new top-level directory that is not listed here only costs
# download size. Nothing else reads these attributes — every other registry gets a built
# package — and they apply from the tree being archived.
/.github          export-ignore
/.gitattributes   export-ignore
/.gitignore       export-ignore
/global.json      export-ignore
/csharp           export-ignore
/rust             export-ignore
/corpus           export-ignore
/docs             export-ignore
/php/tests        export-ignore
/php/phpcs.xml    export-ignore
/php/phpunit.xml  export-ignore

# The one exception: cmd.exe reads a batch file's labels reliably only with CRLF, so the
# Gradle wrapper's gradlew.bat keeps the line endings Gradle wrote it with.
*.bat text eol=crlf
